    "buzz": {
      "word": "BUZZ",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To make a low, continuous, humming or sibilant sound, like that made by bees with their wings. Hence: To utter a murmuring sound; to speak with a low, humming voice. Like a wasp is buzzed, and stung him. Longfellow. However these disturbers of our peace Buzz in the people's ears. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To sound forth by buzzing. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To whisper; to communicate, as tales, in an under tone; to spread, as report, by whispers, or secretly. I will buzz abroad such prophecies That Edward shall be fearful of his life. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To talk to incessantly or confidentially in a low humming voice. [Colloq.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Phonetics)",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To sound with a \"buzz\". H. Sweet.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A continuous, humming noise, as of bees; a confused murmur, as of general conversation in low tones, or of a general expression of surprise or approbation. \"The constant buzz of a fly.\" Macaulay. I found the whole room in a buzz of politics. Addison. There is a buzz all around regarding the sermon. Thackeray.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A whisper; a report spread secretly or cautiously. There's a certain buzz Of a stolen marriage. Massinger.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Phonetics)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The audible friction of voice consonants. H. Sweet.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
